## Workflow conventions

{% include 'workflow-conventions.md' %}

## What to populate

The workflow skill template handles SKILL.md structure, so
the four design fields that matter are:

- `files` — every artifact materialize writes (loom shim,
  Python package, schemas, prompt templates, references).
- `cli_surface` — subcommands the main shim exposes
  (typically `ingest`, `next`, `complete`, plus any
  `pipeline <name>` helpers that tool tasks invoke).
- `tasks` — every loom task in dispatch order. Each
  description is 1-2 sentences at the logical level: what it
  consumes, what it produces, the one decision it makes. Do
  not restate engine wiring (ref / when / skip_output /
  @prev) or list downstream consumers. For a ref-instanced
  fan-out set `ref` on every instance, write the description
  on the first instance only, and give later instances a
  one-line pointer ("instance N of <first-id>").
- `rationale` — at most ~5 sentences or bullets: decisions
  made (DAG shape, fan-out factor, determinism, package
  layout) and alternatives rejected. Nothing else.

`sections` may stay empty — the workflow skill template
supplies standard SKILL.md sections.

## Output construction

Construct `output.yaml` with loom's schema-driven writer.
The writer validates after every assignment.

```bash
LOOM=~/.kiro/skills/home/loom/scripts/loom.sh
WD={{ run.workdir }}

$LOOM output init "$WD" --task design-author

$LOOM output add "$WD" --task design-author \
    --set frontmatter.name=demo \
    --set frontmatter.type=workflow \
    --set 'frontmatter.description=...' \
    --set 'files.0.path=scripts/demo.sh' \
    --set 'files.0.purpose=Loom shim.' \
    --set 'files.0.content_outline=...' \
    --set 'cli_surface.0.command=ingest' \
    --set 'cli_surface.0.args=<args>' \
    --set 'cli_surface.0.description=Initialise the loom workdir.' \
    --set 'cli_surface.1.command=next' \
    --set 'cli_surface.1.args=<workdir>' \
    --set 'cli_surface.1.description=Emit ready tasks.' \
    --set 'tasks.0.id=demo-seed' --set 'tasks.0.kind=tool' \
    --set 'tasks.0.depends_on=[]' \
    --set 'tasks.0.description=Reads ... produces ...' \
    --set 'tasks.1.id=demo-shard-01' --set 'tasks.1.kind=agent' \
    --set 'tasks.1.ref=demo-shard' \
    --set 'tasks.1.depends_on=[demo-seed]' \
    --set 'tasks.1.description=Reviews one shard ... emits findings.' \
    --set 'tasks.2.id=demo-shard-02' --set 'tasks.2.kind=agent' \
    --set 'tasks.2.ref=demo-shard' \
    --set 'tasks.2.depends_on=[demo-seed]' \
    --set 'tasks.2.description=Instance 2 of demo-shard-01.' \
    --set rationale='DAG shape and fan-out tradeoffs...'
```

Syntax: arrays use dotted numeric indices (`files.0.path`,
`tasks.1.id`); `.` nests. The writer validates the whole
document after every `output add`, so required sibling fields
(e.g. `id` + `kind` + `description` of one task) MUST land in
the same call. Schema violations exit non-zero with the
violating path.
